1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,105
Good evening from the OROS closing desk.

2
00:00:03,105 --> 00:00:09,005
This morning the system named no pick, so tonight there is nothing to grade.

3
00:00:09,005 --> 00:00:13,740
No setup, no trade, no loss — that is the discipline working.

4
00:00:13,740 --> 00:00:18,676
Why we grade in public: a forecast that is never checked is just content.

5
00:00:18,676 --> 00:00:24,085
Every morning call gets an evening verdict, and both go in the permanent record.

6
00:00:24,050 --> 00:00:27,566
On the day, the book is up 128 dollars.

7
00:00:27,566 --> 00:00:30,812
BlackRock (BLK) leads, +1.9 percent.

8
00:00:30,812 --> 00:00:33,157
Apple (AAPL) lags at -0.9.

9
00:00:33,157 --> 00:00:38,026
The checklist is clear: nothing requires action today.

10
00:00:38,026 --> 00:00:41,552
On screen, the day's gainers and laggards inside the paper book.

11
00:00:41,508 --> 00:00:49,051
And here is the book itself — the actual holdings, identical to this morning's emailed report.

12
00:00:49,051 --> 00:00:56,273
10 positions, led by AbbVie, ticker ABBV, ConocoPhillips, ticker COP, Chevron, ticker CVX.

13
00:00:56,273 --> 00:01:07,186
Every line carries its share count, its entry, and its exit — because a position without a defined exit is not a position, it is a hope.

14
00:01:07,143 --> 00:01:12,039
Now the part that makes this system different: the loop.

15
00:01:12,039 --> 00:01:14,486
Every morning OROS proposes.

16
00:01:14,486 --> 00:01:25,239
Every evening it grades its own call, journals the verdict, and adjusts its own risk before it is allowed to propose again.

17
00:01:25,239 --> 00:01:29,261
Inside the book today: 7 positions up, 3 down.

18
00:01:29,261 --> 00:01:31,621
Best, BLK at +1.92 percent.

19
00:01:31,621 --> 00:01:33,457
Worst, AAPL at -0.94.

20
00:01:33,457 --> 00:01:38,528
Both go in the journal — wins and losses get the same ink.

21
00:01:38,528 --> 00:01:45,871
The honest scoreboard reads: expectancy -3.04% per trade over 1225 graded positions.

22
00:01:45,871 --> 00:01:53,040
Because that record is soft, the system has throttled itself to 25% position size.

23
00:01:53,040 --> 00:01:58,285
Nobody told it to — full size must be earned back with wins.

24
00:01:58,285 --> 00:02:10,699
What tonight's read hands to tomorrow's open: 0 names close the day in Stage 2; position size stays cut to 25% until the record earns it back.

25
00:02:10,673 --> 00:02:12,902
Over to the sports desk.

26
00:02:12,902 --> 00:02:22,096
Day 85 of the race: three books, the same one hundred thousand simulated dollars, since July sixth.

27
00:02:22,096 --> 00:02:28,318
In first place, the live self-rebuilding book, in blue, at 104,735.

28
00:02:28,318 --> 00:02:33,797
Then the index fund, in gray, 1.7 thousand back at 103,054.

29
00:02:33,797 --> 00:02:40,948
Then the frozen first portfolio, the green line, 6.8 thousand back at 97,906.

30
00:02:40,948 --> 00:02:43,548
The race has no finish line.

31
00:02:43,548 --> 00:02:47,820
Same books, same dollars, back at it tomorrow.

32
00:02:47,820 --> 00:02:52,649
Every line starts the same day with the same dollar.

33
00:02:52,649 --> 00:02:54,506
Nothing is restated.

34
00:02:54,506 --> 00:02:56,836
Today's lesson is benchmarking.

35
00:02:56,836 --> 00:03:03,297
Any strategy must beat the boring alternative: the same money parked in an index fund.

36
00:03:03,297 --> 00:03:06,754
That is why the S and P line shares the chart.

37
00:03:06,754 --> 00:03:15,770
If the system cannot outrun it, the index deserves the money, and publishing the race every day keeps the system honest.

38
00:03:15,729 --> 00:03:23,064
All figures are simulated paper trading, graded and journaled by the OROS agents with no human intervention.

39
00:03:23,064 --> 00:03:25,306
Nothing here is financial advice.

40
00:03:25,306 --> 00:03:28,294
From the closing desk — see you at the open.
